WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.672
A push or a pull is a force. A
force changes how a thing moves.

2
00:00:08.892 --> 00:00:13.954
A force can change a thing's shape, too.
Nothing speeds up or stops by itself.

3
00:00:14.454 --> 00:00:18.495
A still ball sits there for
ever, unless a force acts on it.

4
00:00:18.715 --> 00:00:22.105
A kick is a force. The kick
starts the ball moving.

5
00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:25.158
Push it along as it
rolls, and it speeds up.

6
00:00:25.378 --> 00:00:29.186
Push back against it, and
it slows down and stops.

7
00:00:29.406 --> 00:00:34.328
Tap it on the side, and it turns. It
changes direction: the way it is going.

8
00:00:34.548 --> 00:00:38.589
So a force can start, speed
up, slow, stop or turn a thing.

9
00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:45.358
Here is a toy car on a track. The distance
is how far it rolls. You measure it in steps.

10
00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:49.340
Push it gently. It rolls
a little way: three steps.

11
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.414
A medium push. It rolls
further: six steps.

12
00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:57.999
A hard push! It rolls
furthest of all: nine steps.

13
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.470
Same car, same floor.
Only the push changed.

14
00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.080
There is a pattern: a
bigger push, a bigger move.

15
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.571
Write the three distances in a table.
Gentle, three. Medium, six. Hard, nine.

16
00:01:11.791 --> 00:01:14.996
Then build a block graph,
with one block for each step.

17
00:01:15.216 --> 00:01:19.488
Three blocks for gentle. Six
for medium. Nine for hard.

18
00:01:19.708 --> 00:01:23.052
Look at the columns. Each
one is taller than the last.

19
00:01:23.272 --> 00:01:27.405
That is an increasing pattern:
bigger push, further roll.

20
00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:31.061
An even harder push would
make a taller column still.

21
00:01:31.561 --> 00:01:35.880
A force can change a shape, too.
Squash a sponge, and it goes flat.

22
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.562
Let go, and it springs back into shape.

23
00:01:38.782 --> 00:01:43.426
Bend a metal paperclip. It
changes shape, and it stays bent.

24
00:01:43.646 --> 00:01:48.800
Squash a wooden block. Nothing happens.
Your push is too small to change it.

25
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.667
Forces change shape, as well as movement.

26
00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:56.115
Why does a rolling ball slow
down? It does not run out of go.

27
00:01:56.335 --> 00:02:02.140
The floor rubs against it, all the way along.
That rubbing is a force called friction.

28
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.564
It stops at the wall,
because the wall pushes back.

29
00:02:05.784 --> 00:02:11.311
Nothing speeds up, slows down or turns
by itself. Every change has a cause.

30
00:02:11.811 --> 00:02:15.386
A push or a pull is a
force. It changes movement.

31
00:02:15.606 --> 00:02:19.786
A bigger push makes a bigger
move. The graph shows the pattern.

32
00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:23.675
A force can change a shape
too, like a squashed sponge.

33
00:02:23.895 --> 00:02:30.675
Nothing changes by itself. When a
ball stops, ask: what made it stop?
